// WARFARE AND THE NAVY IN ANCIENT GREECE — English version (same structure and image ids as dados.js).
// Dates are approximate, in the “middle chronology”; those before the Persian Wars rest on traditions fixed centuries later. Numbers of combatants and casualties are estimates from the ancient sources (Herodotus, Thucydides, Xenophon, Diodorus, Polybius, Arrian, Plutarch), often exaggerated; debate is noted where it exists.

const visao = [
  { caixa: 'In brief', texto: [
    'War was such a normal part of Greek life that the city-states (the **poleis**) rarely went a whole generation without it. For centuries the decisive fight was the clash of two tight lines of armed citizens, the **hoplites**, advancing shoulder to shoulder with round shields and long spears, a formation called the **phalanx**. At sea the weapon of choice was the **trireme**, a long, fast warship driven by about 170 oarsmen and armed with a bronze ram at the bow. With hoplites and triremes the Greeks fought the **Persian Wars** (490–479 BC), and Athens and Sparta wore each other down in the **Peloponnesian War** (431–404 BC).',
    'In the fourth century BC warfare changed. Generals such as **Epaminondas** and **Iphicrates** innovated in tactics; mercenaries and light troops gained importance; and **Philip II of Macedon** and his son **Alexander the Great** combined a phalanx of very long spears (the **sarissa**), shock cavalry and siege engines into a professional army that defeated the Persian Empire. Their Hellenistic successors added war elephants and catapults, until Roman legions defeated the phalanx at Cynoscephalae (197 BC) and Pydna (168 BC). This page follows that evolution, with weapons, tactics, ships, sieges and walls, and separates what we know from what is tradition.'
  ] },
  { img: 'ggu-vaso-chigi', leg: 'The Chigi vase, a Proto-Corinthian olpe, c. 650–640 BC: the earliest known image of a hoplite phalanx on the march. Villa Giulia National Etruscan Museum, Rome.' },
  { h: 'War in Greek life' },
  'The Greeks did not separate war from citizenship. In most poleis, a free man who owned land or could afford armour was a soldier, and his right to vote was tied to his duty to fight. Armies were therefore **citizen militias**, not professional forces, called up in spring or summer, after the sowing and before the harvest. Campaigns tended to be short: one army invaded its neighbour’s territory, destroyed crops and hoped the enemy would come out to defend them. If the enemy accepted, the battle was decided in a few hours.',
  'Sparta was the exception. Its full citizens, the **Spartiates**, were trained from childhood and freed from farm work, done by serfs called **helots**; they therefore formed the most feared hoplite army until the fourth century BC. Athens, for its part, made the sea the centre of its power and turned thousands of poor citizens into oarsmen for its fleet.',
  { h: 'Three ages of Greek warfare' },
  { tabela: { cab: ['Age', 'Approximate dates', 'How they fought'], linhas: [
    ['Bronze Age and Dark Age', 'c. 1600 – 750 BC', 'Mycenaean warriors with chariots, spears and large shields; later, bands of warriors on foot with iron weapons. Homer’s picture blends the two periods'],
    ['Hoplite (Archaic and Classical)', 'c. 700 – 360 BC', 'Phalanx of citizens with spear and round shield; cavalry and light troops as auxiliaries; at sea, penteconters and then triremes; sieges mostly by blockade'],
    ['Macedonian and Hellenistic', 'c. 360 – 146 BC', 'Professional or mercenary armies; sarissa, shock cavalry, elephants; catapults and siege towers; ever larger ships; cities with modern walls'],
  ] } },
  { img: 'ggu-trirreme-olympias', leg: 'The *Olympias*, a reconstruction of a fifth-century BC Athenian trireme, built in Greece in 1985–1987 and owned by the Hellenic Navy.' },
  { h: 'Hoplites, triremes and sarissas' },
  'The **hoplite** (Greek *hoplítēs*) takes his name from the *hoplon*, the whole set of equipment, and in particular from the round shield, the *aspis*. About 90 cm across, the shield protected the hoplite and half of the man next to him, so the strength of a phalanx lay in its cohesion. The complete equipment (helmet, cuirass, greaves, shield, spear and sword) weighed, by modern estimates, between 20 and 30 kg, and it cost money: those who owned it belonged to the middle class of landowning farmers.',
  'The **trireme** (Greek *triērēs*) was the great naval invention of the Classical age. It had three superimposed banks of oarsmen, a bronze ram and a crew of about 200 men. In 480 BC, off Salamis, the largest naval battle of Greek antiquity was fought, and it was thanks to a fleet of triremes that Athens dominated the Aegean for almost a century.',
  'The **Macedonian phalanx** that Philip II and Alexander used was different: instead of a spear of about 2 m, the men used the *sarissa*, a spear of 4 to 6 m held in both hands, and carried a smaller shield. The Companion cavalry, in wedge formation, attacked the enemy’s weak point while the phalanx pinned him down.',
  { img: 'ggu-mosaico-alexandre', leg: 'The “Alexander Mosaic”, made c. 120–100 BC (House of the Faun, Pompeii) after a Hellenistic painting of the late fourth or early third century BC; it shows Alexander against Darius, traditionally at Issus (an identification with Gaugamela has also been proposed). National Archaeological Museum, Naples.' },
  { h: 'Rules, rituals and limits' },
  'Greek war was full of ritual. Before battle an animal was sacrificed and the seers read its entrails; the soldiers sang a **paean** (a hymn to Apollo) and advanced at a walk; after victory a **trophy** (*tropaion*) was set up, a post hung with captured arms, at the spot where the enemy had given way. The defeated asked for a **truce** through heralds in order to collect their dead, and whoever refused committed an impiety. Sanctuaries and heralds were in principle inviolable. But these rules did not prevent violence: captured cities saw the men killed and the women and children sold into slavery, as at Scione (421 BC) and Melos (416 BC).',
  { h: 'How we know what we know' },
  'Our main sources are texts by **Herodotus** (the Persian Wars), **Thucydides** and **Xenophon** (the Peloponnesian War and the fourth century), **Diodorus Siculus** (a universal history of the first century BC), **Arrian** and **Plutarch** (on Alexander and other generals, centuries after the events) and **Polybius** (on the Hellenistic period and the comparison between phalanx and legion). To these we add technical treatises (Aeneas Tacticus, Xenophon, Philo of Byzantium), inscriptions, painted pottery, reliefs, and the archaeology of battlefields, armour and walls. Many of the numbers in the sources (Herodotus’ millions of Persians, for example) are exaggerated and debated; modern estimates are always lower.',
  { h: 'Why it matters' },
  { lista: [
    '**Politics and war:** the phalanx was as much a form of social organisation as a military instrument; the idea that those who fight should decide (and that cities poor in land depend on the sea) lies behind both Athenian democracy and Spartan oligarchy.',
    '**Tactics and strategy:** words such as *strategy*, *tactics*, *phalanx*, *logistics* and *trireme* are Greek, and the careful analysis of battles by Thucydides and Xenophon founded military history.',
    '**Technology:** the first catapults, siege towers, the trireme and its ram, and walls with towers and artillery embrasures are Greek inventions or improvements.',
    '**Memory:** Marathon, Thermopylae and Salamis remain symbols of the resistance of a free people, still invoked today, with more or less accuracy.'
  ] },
  { img: 'ggu-falange-choque', leg: 'Artistic reconstruction (made with AI) of the clash of two hoplite phalanxes, c. 420 BC.' },
  { caixa: 'Greek war today', texto: 'Many battlefields can be visited: the Athenian burial mound at **Marathon**, the pass and monument of **Thermopylae**, the Lion of **Chaeronea**, the walls of **Messene** and the Euryalus fort at Syracuse. At **Faliro**, near Athens, the trireme *Olympias* is kept as a museum ship of the Hellenic Navy. The 1896 Olympic Games created the **marathon** race in memory of the legend of the run from Marathon to Athens, which does not come from Herodotus (see the map section).' }
];

const linha = [
  'This timeline follows the evolution of Greek warfare, from the Bronze Age to the Roman conquest. Dates before the Persian Wars are approximate and often traditional; later ones are firm to the year, and sometimes the month. For the wider political context, see the pages on the Classical Age and the Hellenistic World.',
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 1600 – 1200 BC', t: 'Mycenaean warriors', x: 'The Mycenaean kingdoms had a warrior elite with **war chariots**, spears, bronze swords and large shields, as shown by the tombs of Mycenae, the Linear B tablets (from Knossos and Pylos, with lists of chariots and weapons) and the so-called **Dendra panoply** (c. 1400 BC), a suit of bronze plate armour. Fortified palaces such as **Mycenae** and **Tiryns** have “Cyclopean” walls. Homer’s Trojan War recalls this age, but its historicity is debated.' },
    { d: 'c. 1100 – 650 BC', t: 'From the Dark Age to the hoplite phalanx', x: 'With the end of the palaces, armies shrank to groups of warriors on foot with iron weapons; the Homeric poems, perhaps fixed in the eighth century BC, show duels between champions and mass fighting, and historians debate how far they reflect a specific period. Between c. 725 and 650 BC the complete panoply (helmet, bronze cuirass, greaves, round shield with armband and grip, spear) appears in the archaeological record: a tomb at **Argos** (c. 720 BC) held an almost complete panoply, and the **Chigi vase** (c. 650 BC) shows the phalanx advancing. For a long time scholars spoke of a rapid “hoplite revolution”; today many historians see a gradual change over a century or more.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'ggu-couraca-argos', leg: 'The Argos panoply, c. 720 BC: “bell” cuirass and bronze helmet with crest. Archaeological Museum of Argos.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 700 BC', t: 'The trireme', x: 'The first Greek warships were **penteconters**, with fifty oarsmen. According to Thucydides, the Corinthians were the first Greeks to build **triremes**, and the Corinthian Ameinocles made four for Samos. The date (c. 700 BC) is traditional; only in the sixth century BC did the trireme become established, and in the fifth century BC it became the dominant warship.' },
    { d: 'c. 740 – 668 BC (tradition)', t: 'The Messenian wars', x: 'According to tradition, Sparta conquered fertile Messenia in two wars (the first c. 740–720, the second c. 685–668 BC) and reduced the local population to **helot** status. The poet **Tyrtaeus** sang the ideal of the warrior who dies in the front rank. The exact chronology is uncertain, but the enslavement of Messenia shaped Spartan history until 369 BC.' },
    { d: '494 BC', t: 'Lade', x: 'The fleet of the Ionian cities in revolt against Persia (353 triremes, according to Herodotus) was defeated off **Miletus**, after the defection of the Samians and others. Miletus was destroyed. The battle showed the power of the Phoenician navy in Persian service.' },
    { d: '490 BC', t: 'Marathon', x: 'About 9,000 Athenians and 1,000 Plataeans, under **Miltiades** and the polemarch **Callimachus**, defeated a Persian expeditionary force that had landed on the plain of Marathon. According to Herodotus 192 Athenians and 6,400 Persians died. The hoplites apparently ran the last stretch of the advance to avoid the arrows, and strengthened the wings in order to envelop the Persian centre.' }
  ] },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'summer 480 BC', t: 'Thermopylae and Artemisium', x: 'A Greek army of about 7,000 men, commanded by the Spartan king **Leonidas**, held the pass for three days against the army of **Xerxes**. It was outflanked by a mountain path; the king stayed with his 300 Spartans, the 700 Thespians and 400 Thebans (the latter, according to Herodotus, surrendered). At the same time the Greek fleet fought the Persian one at **Artemisium**, without a clear winner, and withdrew.' },
    { d: 'September 480 BC (traditional date)', t: 'Salamis', x: 'On the initiative of **Themistocles**, the Greeks (c. 370 triremes) drew the enormous Persian fleet into the strait between the island of Salamis and Attica, where the heavier ships could not manoeuvre. The Greek victory, narrated by Herodotus and mentioned by Aeschylus (who may have been there), saved Greece.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'ggu-salamina-abalroamento', leg: 'Artistic reconstruction (made with AI) of triremes ramming each other in the strait of Salamis, 480 BC.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '479 BC', t: 'Plataea and Mycale', x: 'An army of united Greeks, under the Spartan **Pausanias**, destroyed the Persian army of **Mardonius** at Plataea, in Boeotia. According to tradition, on the same day a Greek force won at Mycale, in Ionia, and burned the Persian fleet. The Persian threat to mainland Greece was over.' },
    { d: '478/7 – 454 BC', t: 'The Delian League and the Athenian naval empire', x: 'In 478/7 Athens and hundreds of cities created an alliance to continue the war against Persia. The allies paid a tribute (**phóros**) or supplied ships. Around 466 **Cimon** defeated the Persians at the **Eurymedon**. In 454 the League’s treasury was moved to Athens, and the League became, in practice, an Athenian empire.' },
    { d: 'c. 461 – 446 BC', t: 'The Long Walls and the first war with Sparta', x: 'Athens linked the city to its port at **Piraeus** with two parallel walls (the **Long Walls**, about 6 km long), so that it could never be besieged by land and could be supplied by sea. An intermittent war with Sparta and Corinth followed (the “First Peloponnesian War”), ended by the peace of 446/5.' },
    { d: '431 BC', t: 'The Peloponnesian War begins', x: 'Athens, with a fleet of about 300 triremes, and Sparta, with its league of allies, fought for 27 years. **Pericles’** strategy was to avoid battle in the open field, gather the population behind the Long Walls and use the fleet to attack enemy coasts. The plague of 430–426 killed many Athenians, including Pericles himself (429).' },
    { d: '429 BC', t: 'Naupactus: Phormio and the Athenian oarsmen', x: 'With only 20 ships, the Athenian admiral **Phormio** defeated 47 Peloponnesian ships off Rhium, thanks to the better manoeuvring of his oarsmen, and repeated the feat soon after, near Naupactus, against a fleet of 77. It is the high point of Athenian naval superiority, described by Thucydides.' },
    { d: '425 BC', t: 'Sphacteria: the Spartans surrender', x: 'After a storm and the fortification of Pylos by **Demosthenes**, about 420 hoplites (some 120 of them Spartiates) were cut off on the island of Sphacteria and the 292 survivors surrendered. It was a shock: Spartans “never surrendered”. The prisoners served as hostages for years.' }
  ] },
  { linha: [
    { d: '424 BC', t: 'Delium: the 25-deep phalanx', x: 'At Delium, in Boeotia, the Thebans deepened their right wing to 25 men (instead of the usual 8) and defeated the Athenians. Thucydides, who describes the battle, gives an early example of the tactic that Epaminondas perfected.' },
    { d: '415 – 413 BC', t: 'The Sicilian expedition', x: 'Athens sent a great fleet and army to besiege **Syracuse**. With Spartan help the Syracusans resisted, destroyed the Athenian fleet in the Great Harbour and captured thousands of men, who were left in the city’s quarries. Two generals, Nicias and Demosthenes, were executed. It was the greatest disaster in Athenian history.' },
    { d: '405 – 404 BC', t: 'Aegospotami and the surrender of Athens', x: 'The Spartan **Lysander**, with Persian money, surprised the Athenian fleet beached on the coast of the Chersonese at Aegospotami and captured about 170 ships; the prisoners were executed. Blockaded by sea and by land, Athens surrendered in 404 BC. The Long Walls were torn down to the sound of flutes.' },
    { d: '401 – 399 BC', t: 'The Ten Thousand', x: 'More than 10,000 Greek hoplites, in the pay of **Cyrus the Younger**, fought at Cunaxa, near Babylon (401 BC). With the prince dead, the Greeks had to march home on foot, through Mesopotamia and Armenia, to the Black Sea, under generals elected by the soldiers, among them **Xenophon**, who told the story in the *Anabasis*. It proved that an army of mercenaries could defeat, and cross, the lands of the Great King.' },
    { d: 'c. 399 BC', t: 'Syracuse invents the catapult', x: 'The tyrant **Dionysius I** gathered engineers from many cities to prepare for war against Carthage and, according to Diodorus, the first **catapults** came from them (arrow-shooting engines, the *katapeltikon*), together with larger ships with four and five banks of oars. The heavy crossbow that preceded them, the *gastraphetes*, is earlier and is described by Heron, with no known inventor. Torsion catapults are slightly later (mid-fourth century BC).' },
    { d: '390 BC', t: 'Lechaeum: peltasts defeat a Spartan mora', x: 'Near Corinth, the Athenian **Iphicrates** attacked with peltasts (light infantry) a column of Spartan hoplites without support, and killed about 250. The victory showed that the heavy phalanx was vulnerable to missiles when it lacked cavalry and light troops in support.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'ggu-formacao-leuctra', leg: 'Diagram (made with AI) of Epaminondas’ oblique order at Leuctra, 371 BC: Theban left wing in depth; centre and right held back.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '371 – 362 BC', t: 'Leuctra and Mantinea: Thebes and Epaminondas', x: 'At **Leuctra** (371 BC) **Epaminondas** put the Theban left wing, 50 men deep and with the Sacred Band, to attack the Spartans’ strongest point (where King **Cleombrotus** stood), while the rest of the line held back. Sparta lost about 400 of its c. 700 Spartiates present. In the following years the Thebans invaded Laconia and founded **Messene** (369) and **Megalopolis**. At **Mantinea** (362) Epaminondas won, but was mortally wounded.' },
    { d: '359 – 338 BC', t: 'Philip II and the new Macedon', x: 'Philip II reorganised the Macedonian army: he created a professional infantry phalanx with the sarissa and a strong cavalry, and used siege engineers. He defeated the Greeks at **Chaeronea** (338 BC), where he destroyed the Sacred Band of Thebes, and created the League of Corinth, the alliance under his leadership. He was assassinated in 336 BC.' },
    { d: '334 – 326 BC', t: 'Alexander: from the Granicus to the Hydaspes', x: 'Alexander defeated the Persians at the **Granicus** (334), at **Issus** (333) and at **Gaugamela** (331), took **Tyre** after a seven-month siege (332) and led his army as far as India, where he defeated King **Porus** at the **Hydaspes** (326), though the elephants were against him. His soldiers refused to go further, and he turned back.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'ggu-gaugamela-cavalaria', leg: 'Artistic reconstruction (made with AI) of a charge by the Macedonian Companion cavalry in wedge formation, Gaugamela, 331 BC.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '323 – 301 BC', t: 'The Diadochi and the elephants', x: 'After Alexander’s death his generals fought for forty years over the pieces of the empire. At **Ipsus** (301 BC) **Seleucus** and **Lysimachus** defeated Antigonus and his son Demetrius; Seleucus brought some 400 to 500 elephants (the numbers vary), obtained in India, and they were decisive. In 306 BC Demetrius had defeated Ptolemy at sea off **Salamis in Cyprus**.' },
    { d: '305 – 304 BC', t: 'The siege of Rhodes', x: 'Demetrius, nicknamed “Poliorcetes” (the “Besieger of Cities”), attacked Rhodes with a giant assault tower, the **Helepolis**, and other engines, without managing to take the city. According to tradition, the Rhodians sold the siege equipment left by the enemy and with the proceeds raised the **Colossus of Rhodes**.' },
    { d: '217 BC', t: 'Raphia: elephants against elephants', x: 'At Raphia, near Gaza, the Seleucid king **Antiochus III** (102 Indian elephants) faced the Egyptian king **Ptolemy IV** (73 African elephants) with some 60,000 and 70,000 infantry respectively, according to Polybius. The African elephants fled, and Ptolemy won. It is the largest Hellenistic battle of which we have a detailed description.' },
    { d: '197 – 168 BC', t: 'Rome defeats the phalanx', x: 'At **Cynoscephalae** (197), at **Magnesia** (190, against Antiochus III) and at **Pydna** (168, against Perseus of Macedon), the more flexible Roman legions defeated the Hellenistic phalanxes. Polybius and Livy explain: the phalanx works only on level ground; the legion adapts.' },
    { d: '146 BC', t: 'Corinth and the end of free Greece', x: 'After the war with the Achaean League, the consul **Mummius** destroyed **Corinth** and Greece came under Roman rule. In the following centuries Greek warfare was above all studied, and imitated, by others.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'ggu-monumento-paulo-emilio', leg: 'Pillar of Lucius Aemilius Paullus at Delphi, c. 167 BC, with a frieze depicting an episode of the battle of Pydna.' }
];

